the US Navy called off the search this  morning for three sailors missing in the  western Pacific they were on a cargo  plane that crashed on Wednesday trying  to reach an American aircraft carrier  south of Japan Ben Tracy is following  the story from Beijing Ben good morning  good morning so the Navy ended the  search at 10 a.m. local time and they  are now notifying the families of these  lost sailors in all there were 11  members of the Navy that were on board a  c2 cargo plane known as a greyhound  when it crashed in the Philippine Sea  Wednesday afternoon about 575 miles  southeast of Okinawa Japan eight of the  sailors were quickly rescued the plane  was on a routine run out to the USS  Ronald Reagan aircraft carrier which was  participating in joint naval exercises  with Japanese forces at the time during  a two-day search eight ships and three  helicopter squadrons cover nearly 1,000  square nautical miles of ocean searching  for the missing sailors but they were  not found these three deaths now add to  the 17 other members of the Navy's  seventh fleet who were killed during  collisions involving the USS Fitzgerald  and McCain earlier this year in both of  those incidents the ship's crew was  found to be at fault now the Navy is  investigating the cause of this latest  accident but they have acknowledged that  the high tempo of operations in the  Pacific has led to reduced safety  margins Tony Benn Traci forests in  Beijing been thank you very much
 

